A word has a public meaning and a private history. The public meaning gives us something to check together. The private history shapes what we hear first.

Take “professional.” One person hears polished and formal. Another hears prepared, precise, and reliable. So if you ask me to make an email more professional, I could make it sound more corporate while leaving the actual problem untouched. If what you meant was “make the ask clear and give them a deadline,” we can both read the rewrite and see whether it did that. That’s where a shared standard helps.

Shrolden lets us watch a private meaning form. Play these out loud without defining the word first.

By lunch, the whole office had gone shrolden.

The boards were already shrolden when we pulled them out of the creek.

He got shrolden every time his brother brought up the trip.

I hear tense and quiet, waterlogged and warped, and withdrawn. Same word each time. And if the office sentence is the one you heard first, you might still hear a trace of that silence in the other two.

That brings it back to montage. A sentence cuts from a word to a scene. The first scene gives the word a meaning to carry into the next one. That’s a fun test for Pantheon’s empty slots. Give two people the same invented word in different scenes, then have them each use it in a new sentence. The sentence they invent tells you what they carried forward.